The problem asks us to calculate the sum of $f(P)$ over all permutations $P$ of the sequence $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$, modulo 998244353.
Here, $f(P)$ is formed by concatenating the decimal representations of the elements of $P$ and interpreting the result as an integer.

Let $P = (P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N)$ be a permutation of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$.
$f(P)$ is the number formed by concatenating $P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N$.
Let $L_i$ be the number of digits in the decimal representation of $i$.
Then $f(P)$ can be written as:
$f(P) = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
Wait, this is not quite right. The value is formed by concatenation.
Let's look at the contribution of each number $x \in \{1, \ldots, N\}$ to the total sum.
Suppose $x$ is placed at position $k$ in the permutation $P$ (i.e., $P_k = x$).
The digits of $x$ will occupy positions in the final number corresponding to the place values determined by the lengths of the numbers following $x$.
Specifically, if $x$ is at index $k$, then the digits of $x$ contribute to the sum based on the powers of 10.
Let $S$ be the string formed by concatenating $P_1, \ldots, P_N$.
The last digit of $P_N$ is at position $10^0$.
The last digit of $P_{N-1}$ is at position $10^{L_{P_N}}$.
In general, the digits of $P_k$ contribute to the sum multiplied by $10^{\sum_{j=k+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.

We need to compute $\sum_{P} f(P) \pmod{998244353}$.
By linearity of expectation (or just summing over positions), we can swap the summation order.
Total Sum $= \sum_{P} \sum_{k=1}^N P_k \times 10^{\sum_{j=k+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
$= \sum_{k=1}^N \sum_{P} P_k \times 10^{\sum_{j=k+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.

Let's fix the position $k$ in the permutation. We want to sum $P_k \times 10^{\sum_{j=k+1}^N L_{P_j}}$ over all permutations.
The term $P_k$ can be any value $v \in \{1, \ldots, N\}$.
If $P_k = v$, then the remaining $N-1$ elements are a permutation of $\{1, \ldots, N\} \setminus \{v\}$.
The exponent $\sum_{j=k+1}^N L_{P_j}$ depends only on the set of elements placed at positions $k+1, \ldots, N$.
Let $S_{k+1, N}$ be the set of values $\{P_{k+1}, \ldots, P_N\}$. This is a subset of size $N-k$ from $\{1, \ldots, N\}$.
For a fixed subset $U \subset \{1, \ldots, N\}$ with $|U| = N-k$, how many permutations have $\{P_{k+1}, \ldots, P_N\} = U$?
The elements in $U$ can be arranged in $(N-k)!$ ways in positions $k+1, \ldots, N$.
The element at position $k$ is fixed to be $v \notin U$.
The elements in $\{1, \ldots, N\} \setminus (U \cup \{v\})$ are arranged in positions $1, \ldots, k-1$ in $(k-1)!$ ways.
So for a fixed $v$ and fixed subset $U$ of size $N-k$ not containing $v$, the number of permutations is $(N-k)! (k-1)!$.
The contribution of such a configuration to the sum is $v \times 10^{\sum_{u \in U} L_u}
